WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.660
California games!

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.600
Now the summer will surely last forever!

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:27.400
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.440
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.860
And that's it.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.280
You know, but the crew will surely last forever.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.600
Right, Sean? I think nothing can tear us apart.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.780
Never, never gonna tear us apart.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.320
There you go. Beautiful in excess song.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.960
But also, you know, just want to go back to that back cover for a second of the summer will surely last forever.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:54.020
It's a bit poetic as we are seeing, you know, the end of the summer now in 2021.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:02.880
But California Games promises us that as long as we have the cartridge in our consoles, if we're playing in winter, it's practically summer.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:09.420
Yeah, this game, it does a very medium job at creating a vibe.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.280
And I think that's worth talking about.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:21.740
Yeah, so I don't want to, like, pretend that this is Grand Theft Auto San Andreas, you know, picturing of California in any way, shape, or form.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:30.140
But, you know, without talking about the actual gameplay, do you feel like they sold you on the idea of, like, this is happening in California and has California vibes?

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.360
Have you yourself been to California, Sean?

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.000
I've never been to California.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.120
It's on my list.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:46.220
But I think that, you know, the pop culture idea of California is pretty ingrained in everybody's heads.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:53.480
You know, like, they tried to make the title card the license plate, but they didn't get the font right.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.440
So I'm like, that's off to a bad start.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:02.220
Like, they had the idea, like, they had the right idea, but they just didn't quite execute on that title card.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:10.080
Yeah, I don't know what it is, but I think you hit it on the, you know, you hit it on the head there when you said the pop culture image of California.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.240
That's really what this is.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:19.180
Like, I don't think, like, anything in this game sells me on the idea that it might be taking place on a particular beach in California,

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.040
or, like, you know, that these are things exclusive to California.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:28.800
But in the late 80s, you know, especially with, like, Saved by the Bell and stuff like that, like,

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.240
I think this is people's idea of California.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:44.720
And now, you know, if you're in New York or rural Oklahoma, like, you can have that feeling of being in California encapsulized in sports video game format.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.400
Yeah, I guess you could say that.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.080
That's very generous of you.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.540
Very generous of me.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:59.420
But I guess what I meant is, is, like, when you play a baseball game, right, and, you know, you play at a California stadium, right,

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:09.100
like for the Los Angeles Angels or Dodgers or something, you never feel like, oh, I'm in California now, even though that's, like, literally a real location in California.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:14.860
Yeah, this, you know, they've got the famous skateboarders of Los Angeles.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.000
They've got the famous hacky sackers of San Francisco.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:25.240
You surf at the beach, which I think is a town in California.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.580
It's, yeah, it basically hits all the strokes there.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:40.780
Yeah. And like you said, this is basically just another one of those games where we go down the line and play a series of little sports mini games that are all under the same theme.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:47.300
So this is made by Epix, and they made, though, they made winter games, which we absolutely hated.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.880
They made summer games, which we liked.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:55.700
And I guess now they're like, hey, how about California games?

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:02.420
I'm not sure where spring and winter went or why California was chosen over any location.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:03.460
California season, yeah.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.760
Right. But don't you remember, we also played world games, and I feel like that could have just included California.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:19.600
But they doubled down on the idea that most of these sports happen exclusively in California or that they're trying to encapsulate what California is via sports.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:23.400
I think you couldn't have said it better right there.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.020
So how do we feel about these games, though, right?

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:29.260
Like, I mean, we all know that we didn't like winter games.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.700
That episode's pretty famous now.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.400
But like these games seem to just be...

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.720
Mini games. Mini games or, yeah, exactly.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.040
Like, I don't know. How do you play these games, Sean?

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:46.920
Do you actually like go through the gauntlet of going through each of them and gaining your scores and checking out what your high scores at the end?

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:53.880
Or do you just say, like, I'm just going to play each mode and I kind of, you know, just like go into practice mode or tackle each one individually?

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.900
Have you ever experienced the full game from beginning to end in one of these?

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:06.180
Yeah, that's how I usually do these because it's kind of a genre in itself now, even though I think, yeah, that one company made all of them.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:12.320
Right. So, yeah, I'll play through the whole tournament with no...

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.540
Like, I think the previous games, you could be the only contestant.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.320
But in this one, you can be the only contestant.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:28.620
And that basically just serves as like a demo for each mode because you win the trophy each time.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.180
But, yeah, so I did one by one.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:48.920
If there's a game that I think is a little bit deeper or at least a little bit more interesting, I will, after I do the little sampler, I'll go back into a practice mode or maybe or just like the single event mode and play around with that.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:50.680
So I did that in here as well.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.500
And so let's just go down the line here.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:59.440
We've got skateboarding up first where, you know, not, I guess, kind of like Skate or Die, right?

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.880
Skate or Die was like a whole game based around skateboarding.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.600
But in this one, you have that half pipe mode, which is kind of similar.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:06.780
But the controls are a lot different.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.260
Sean, what did you think of the controls here for skateboarding?

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:19.640
They were, I think, if I remember Skate or Die correctly, did it have a half pipe?

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.560
Yeah, it did have a half pipe.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:31.320
Okay. I think I was able to perform a bit better in the previous one because I don't remember being as frustrated by a half pipe as in this one.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:37.760
I was able to, like, get some speed going because, like, your controls kind of reverse.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:47.660
Like, you've got to hold up and down in, like, a rhythmic fashion to gain some speed, which is kind of cool.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.920
Like, it feels a little bit more tactile that way.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:57.220
But then when you try and do almost any trick, I just kept falling and eating dirt.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:03.900
I was able to do a couple of, like, the hand stalls kind of accidentally.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:08.720
But if I tried to do any kind of spin, I wasn't doing very good.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.260
It's very hard to pull off tricks in this mode.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.500
These games always seem to have that, though, Sean.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:23.060
You know, like, there's always a weird timing mechanic that just doesn't translate well for me in any of these Epix games.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:35.960
Like, that's their biggest downfall is that, you know, like, let's talk about you saying with the momentum of your skateboarding and how you have to, like, get higher is by when you're going up to half pipe, you hold the up button on the D-pad.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:37.700
And when you go down, you hold down.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:39.720
Now, that's kind of like it's like a pendulum thing.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:43.000
Like, you have to think about where you are on the pendulum for that.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.320
And that made sense to me.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:49.100
And, you know, at first I was like, oh, well, they could have just, you know, done that for me, I suppose.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:53.020
But I liked having control over how much speed I was going to have.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.160
I could only do the kick turns in this one.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:57.660
You could do the kick turns.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.880
Yes. And that's the only thing I was able to do successfully.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:07.100
I was not able to even, you know, hang like you were on the top of the half pipe or anything like that.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:13.080
Every time I tried that, it seemed like I was, you know, they let you get the act of performing it.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.260
It shows that you've put the buttons in.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:16.540
But you always bail.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:21.780
Right. You always bail. You just didn't do the right thing at the last second, whatever that was supposed to be.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.120
Very frustrating experience.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.620
But I've noticed this a lot over and over again.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:31.060
So I don't know what it comes down to here for this kind of thing.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.140
Should they have just given you more leeway on that?

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.360
And therefore, like, yeah, everybody scores lots of points.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:44.380
But then it becomes like a risk or reward factor where, like, you know, you're not just rewarded for scoring points.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.580
You have to score a certain amount of points or, like, take bigger leaps, you know?

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.180
Like, I'm not sure what it is.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:56.260
But I don't think, you know, like, performing tricks should have been so hard.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.840
Because that's, like, the entirety of this event.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:02.600
You're not thrilled by just going up and down on the half pipe.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:07.900
I feel like that's the standard experience that we would expect movement in our games.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.080
I think the tricks is what you want to do.

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:19.980
And so the fact that that becomes kind of a, you know, a hard thing to pull off makes this particular skateboarding game, you know, not as fun as it could be.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.320
Because you could just add more different kinds of tricks or more different inputs.

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:29.180
But they should have given a larger, you know, input time to pull it off.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:30.560
Yeah, I agree.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:35.060
I want to know what your secret was for doing the kick turns.

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:45.620
Because any time I tried, because I was looking at the diagram in the manual for, like, oh, this is what you do at this point in order to pull off this trick.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:47.500
And I thought I was doing it correctly.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:55.940
But every time I pressed the button to do this kick turn, which I don't think anybody actually calls.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.680
I don't know. I thought it was just turning around.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.520
Yeah, I just fell off the board.

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.780
And the board went in a different direction as me.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.720
And, yeah, so I want to know how you did that.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:10.060
I guess you want to know how I did the hand stall.

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:11.940
It was just, I guess we had different rhythms.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.140
Yeah. Well, I'll tell you, I don't know how it works for the hand stalls or not.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:37.400
But for the kick turns, because of, you know, that happens at the lower half of the half pipe, kind of before you start, before your body is vertical, you have to kind of do that where as you're going up the ramp, like beforehand, before you're going up the ramp, that's when you do the first half of the input.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:40.880
And then as you're turning around, you have to change the input.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:43.700
It's not like a one-two.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:44.820
I see.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:49.260
It's like a one-two, you know, which made sense to me.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:11:04.960
But maybe that's why I wasn't able to pull off the air tricks, because every time I tried to do those, I felt like I was just, you know, not reactive enough, because it was basically showing me that no matter what I was going to do, I was going to screw up.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.560
Like, I had lost control of my board.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:13.060
I guess in this way, it's very similar to when I tried to skateboard when I was, like, 13 years old.

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:16.780
Yeah. You think you're going to just go out there and, like, you know.

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:17.840
Tony Hawk it up. Right.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:20.600
Yeah. Yeah, like, it's like, yeah, yeah, Tony Hawk it up.

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.340
But, like, even just, like, an ollie, you're like, all right, I'll start with the basics, whatever.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:25.820
And then you just never do that.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:27.500
You never just pour it in the air.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.540
I did. I was able to ollie.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:35.260
That's nice. It wasn't very dependable, but I could do it once every three times.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:37.820
So what's your air trick trick?

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:40.100
My air trick?

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.800
You know, like, in the game, what was your trick to doing the air tricks?

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:46.620
I couldn't. Oh, I'm sorry.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.180
I didn't mean to disappoint you.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.820
Oh. The handstand thing or whatever.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:56.680
Yeah. I guess I just felt whatever rhythm they wanted me to feel.

00:11:57.260 --> 00:12:06.880
And I just sort of held the button for as long as it felt like a person would be able to hold that position.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.720
And then I let go and it worked.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:13.340
So I have no idea how to describe it.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.280
Yeah, I don't know.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:22.500
There's something about, like, skate or die communicating better what you're doing and having fun with it, too.

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:24.500
But they also had a wider variety of tricks.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.100
So maybe, I don't know.

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:30.140
You know, it's only one game of, like, seven in California games.

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:33.720
So maybe they didn't have enough time to, like, put a bunch into the skateboarding.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:39.680
But I would think, you know, skateboarding to me is, like, that feels like a, you know, I'm not sure about this.

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:41.220
But it feels like it was invented in California.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:43.940
So, like, why not go all out if this is California games?

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:45.660
Why not go all out on the skateboarding?

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:47.580
And they just didn't, you know?

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:50.580
I'm almost positive that you're correct there.

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:58.580
I think it very much was a Los Angeles outer, like, the outskirts of Los Angeles sort of thing.

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.080
Yeah, the Lord's a Dogtown.

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:02.180
Yeah. All right.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:03.720
You feel good about skateboarding?

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.720
I feel good about skateboarding.

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:29.600
Great, because now I want to talk to you about a little game, you know, well-known game called Footbag.

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:32.320
I just don't know.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:34.620
You know, maybe they do call it that on the West Coast.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:36.340
Maybe that's, like, a different thing, you know?

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:37.800
Like, maybe they call it Footbag.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:40.120
Have you ever heard it at all like that?

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:41.600
I've never heard that term.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:43.160
I only know Hacky Sack.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.960
Yeah. Maybe that's an East Coast thing.

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:47.020
I don't know. That's what I'm saying.

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.180
This is an East Coast-West Coast rivalry right now.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:53.700
Yeah. Footbag, to me, sounds like they're trying to make it into a sport, though.

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:55.440
You know, like, it's close enough to football.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:56.640
They're like, this is competitive.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:02.260
Hacky Sack is a game you play with your friends where it's, like, four square or something like that.

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:06.740
Yeah. This, this footbag is, like, competitive freestyle Hacky Sack.

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:13.040
Yeah. When you look at this guy, he looks like an acrobat.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:14.780
Like, he looks so serious.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.520
He's in, like, he's got, like, a technique.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:21.860
He's got a stance that I wouldn't really associate with Hacky Sack.

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:27.900
Um, and this actually was one of the, uh, this felt better.

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:33.820
Like, it almost felt good to play this game because the, the bag seems to have some physics to it.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.240
Uh, so I, I thought it was neat.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:38.660
Yeah, yeah, I agree with you.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:43.240
Uh, the bag has some physics to the sense that, like, it won't just keep going straight up.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:44.740
That would be really boring.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:50.960
Yeah. Um, so it does, like, even if you hit it straight on, it still does move ever so slightly to the left or right.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:52.020
Uh, which is good.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:53.780
I don't know how they calculate that, but whatever.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:58.580
And then, um, you know, again, the timing here is more forgiving.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:05.020
Um, if you, you know, if you want to hit it on your head or whatever, but miss, you still have a chance to, like, hit it with your foot.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:11.380
You know, or, um, even hitting it with your foot isn't, like, this precise, uh, input.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:12.560
Yeah, there's, like, a hitbox.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:21.020
Right, exactly. As long as your foot is up in the air and makes contact with the, the bag, doesn't really matter what happens next.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.840
But, you know, the best part about this game is all the different tricks you can do.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:29.200
Again, this is, like, learning everything that skateboarding didn't do.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:38.740
Because I was, like, blown away by just, like, discovering new moves in a game that I thought really only had, like, you kick the bag and you hit it with your head.

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:44.620
When I started, like, jumping and doing back, uh, you know, back turns, like, you could do 180s or...

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:46.160
I think that's called the Dizzy Dean.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:48.040
Yeah, oh, you remember.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:53.300
You, I was gonna try and think about, like, putting up the terms on the, on the dock here.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:56.020
But I was like, wow, there's just so many, it'd be a waste of my time.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:58.440
I know, they're really, they're really funny.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:00.400
There's the jester that I got.

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.200
There's, I got, like, the full axle.

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:08.460
Apparently, there's, like, a readout of all the, uh, of, of all the tricks that you did.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:12.840
Uh, but it also includes all the tricks that exist, which I think is a little presumptuous.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:20.520
Um, uh, it kind of reduces the amount of creativity that this board of footbag can, can, uh, innovate.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:26.820
Uh, but I was only able to do, like, three or four of the tricks, and I don't even know what half the other ones are.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:35.440
Well, most of them are just, like, a combination of, uh, flipping your, uh, what do you want to, how do you say that?

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:39.760
Like, when you, when you jump, and now your back is turned to the screen.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:41.560
Like, is it a... Oh, a 180 jump.

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:43.080
A 180 ground jump.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:47.740
Most of these are 180 ground jump combinations with something else.

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:52.360
Um, and then also, like, it's a combination of where your foot is, too, though.

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:53.600
Because if you notice...

00:16:53.600 --> 00:17:00.220
